# XSH pthread_mutexattr_getprioceiling(), pthread_mutexattr_setprioceiling()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# The default ceiling is not specified; an out-of-range ceiling and a
# missing privilege are may-fail errors. The ceilings used are the lowest
# and the highest SCHED_FIFO priority, the range the page names.

assertion  XSH.pthread_mutexattr_getprioceiling.001
anchor     functions/pthread_mutexattr_getprioceiling.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/pthread_mutexattr_getprioceiling.html#RETURN VALUE
class      conditional
when       RPP|TPP
since      2001
says       pthread_mutexattr_setprioceiling() with the lowest and then
           the highest SCHED_FIFO priority returns 0, and
           pthread_mutexattr_getprioceiling() returns 0 and stores that
           priority each time.
note       EINVAL or EPERM from the set leave the test UNRESOLVED.
test       t/001-round-trip.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_mutexattr_getprioceiling.002
anchor     functions/pthread_mutexattr_getprioceiling.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       RPP|TPP
since      2001
says       A mutex initialized with an attributes object whose protocol is
           PTHREAD_PRIO_PROTECT and whose priority ceiling is the highest
           SCHED_FIFO priority has that ceiling:
           pthread_mutex_getprioceiling() returns 0 and stores it.
note       A refusal of the protocol or of the mutex (EPERM, ENOTSUP,
           EINVAL) leaves the test UNRESOLVED.
test       t/002-mutex-ceiling.c
